    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Education has long been a source of hope in the face of social problems. When climate change\r\nemerged as a preeminent issue of the twenty-first century, climate and environmental educational\r\ncampaigns sprung up in response. Scholars have explored the impacts of climate education on\r\nemotional outcomes for students and sustainability outcomes for society, and the impacts of\r\ncurriculum on all of the above. However, little empirical attention has been given to how social\r\ncontexts and teachers' adaptations to these contexts reshape these outcomes. Focusing on\r\nelementary and middle schools in liberal areas where climate change is mostly accepted as fact, I\r\ndraw on 13 interviews with educators in various social contexts to document why and how teachers\r\nteach about climate change and the environment. I find that educators across socioeconomic\r\ncontexts share a goal to instill their students with hope and capacity to be changemakers; however,\r\nthis goal manifests itself differently in accordance with social and environmental contexts of\r\ncommunities. Advantaged communities had easier access to natural spaces which thus played a\r\nsignificant role in teacher approaches to education; meanwhile, disadvantaged communities had\r\nlittle access to natural spaces, and teachers instead engaged with the local climate injustices that\r\ndisproportionately harmed their communities. When students come from socioeconomically\r\nadvantaged backgrounds, their teachers were more concerned about mitigating emotional harms,\r\nand approached instilling hope through connection to nature and large-scale climate solutions. In\r\ndisadvantaged communities, teachers were less concerned about students being emotionally\r\nimpacted by climate change, and they instilled hope by empowering their students to fight against\r\nenvironmental injustice in their own communities. In outlining how socioeconomic context\r\nreshapes how teachers approach generating hope amongst their students, this study contributes to\r\nliterature on climate change, education, inequality, and hope.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
